  "raw_text": "Step 1: For the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{2-x}{\\log(2-\\log(x-2))} \\) to be defined, the argument of the logarithm must be positive, and the denominator cannot be zero.\n\nStep 2: First, consider the condition for the logarithm inside the denominator to be defined: \\(2 - \\log(x-2) > 0\\).\nStep 3: Solving \\(2 - \\log(x-2) > 0\\) gives \\(\\log(x-2) < 2\\), which implies \\(x-2 < 10^2 = 100\\). Therefore, \\(x < 102\\).\n\nStep 4: Next, ensure that the argument of the inner logarithm is positive: \\(x-2 > 0\\), which simplifies to \\(x > 2\\).\n\nStep 5: Additionally, the denominator itself must not be zero, so \\(2 - \\log(x-2) \\neq 1\\). This means \\(\\log(x-2) \\neq 1\\), which implies \\(x-2 \\neq 10\\), or \\(x \\neq 12\\).\n\nStep 6: Combining all these conditions, we get \\(2 < x < 102\\) and \\(x \\neq 12\\).\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{(2, 12) \\cup (12, 102)}<|im_end|>",
